mode: "100644"
text: "# Why Things Spread\n\nVirality is about **shares** — people sending content to other people — far more than passive views.\nSo the central question in any teardown is: *why did someone share this?* This is the psychology\nbehind the share-trigger layer, grounded in established research on why content spreads.\n\n> These are durable principles from research on virality (e.g., Berger & Milkman's work on\n> high-arousal emotion, and the social-transmission factors popularized as STEPPS). Paraphrased and\n> applied here; verify nothing changes about human psychology quarterly (it won't).\n\n## The share-triggers\n\nPeople share for a handful of recurring reasons. Most viral content hits one or more **hard**:\n\n- **Identity / self-expression** — \"this is so me,\" or \"this says what I believe.\" People share to\n  signal who they are. The strongest trigger: content that lets the sharer express their identity or\n  values *to their audience*.\n- **High-arousal emotion** — emotions that activate the body drive sharing: **awe, anger, anxiety,\n  excitement, amusement, inspiration.** Low-arousal emotions (mild contentment, sadness) spread\n  less. The intensity matters more than positivity — anger spreads, mild \"nice\" doesn't.\n- **Social currency** — sharing it makes the sharer look good: smart, funny, in-the-know, ahead of\n  the curve. People pass on things that enhance how others see them.\n- **Practical value** — genuinely useful enough that you send it to a specific person who needs it\n  (\"you have to see this\"). Save-and-share utility.\n- **Relatability** — \"it me.\" Naming a shared, slightly-unspoken experience so precisely that people\n  tag their friends (\"this is literally you\").\n- **Triggers / topicality** — tied to something already on people's minds (a season, an event, a\n  recurring moment) so it gets re-shared whenever that cue appears.\n- **Story** — wrapped in a narrative people retell, carrying the message (or brand) along with it.\n\n## Using the triggers in a teardown\n\nWhen you reach the share-trigger layer, name the **specific** trigger(s) at work — don't stop at\n\"it's engaging.\" Examples:\n\n- A founder's \"I quit my job\" video → **identity** (viewers project their own dream) + **inspiration**\n  (high-arousal) + **story**.\n- A \"things clients say vs mean\" post → **relatability** (\"it me\") + **social currency** (sharing it\n  signals you're in the know).\n- A sharp how-to → **practical value** (sent to a colleague) + maybe **social currency**.\n\nThe trigger is usually the real driver the counterfactual test surfaces. A piece with a competent\nhook but **no share-trigger** gets views, not virality — that's why it plateaus.\n\n## The honest caveat\n\nHitting a trigger **stacks the odds**; it doesn't guarantee a share-storm. Distribution, timing, and\nluck still decide outcomes (see `replicability-and-application.md`). The triggers tell you *why*\nsomething spread and *how* to give your content a real chance — not how to manufacture a guaranteed\nhit.\n"
